### Page 4 of 9

```text
Final Plat/UDC-15-00425
3
EXECUTIVE SUMMARY:

This final plat application is for a replat to ninety (90) residential lots on the southeast corner of
the intersection of US Highway 30 and Whitney Road. This application was submitted in
conjunction with a future land use map amendment request and a zone change application to
MR-2. The preliminary plat application was approved July 2015 by the Governing Body. Originally,
this area was platted into commercial lots but now, the owner would like to sell this area as
residential units.

This final plat is substantially similar to the preliminary plat that was submitted. The donation
language on the plat has not been changed as requested by the Parks and Recreation
Department during the preliminary plat phase. Additional connections to existing streets have
also not been included as recommended by multiple departments. It was also recommended that
the name Wilderness Court be changed to avoid confusion with Wilderness Trail. These
comments, along with a few technical changes to the map, were expected to be addressed with
the submittal of the final plat though they remain unresolved.

Due to meeting the review criteria of Section 2.1.3.d of the Cheyenne Unified
Development Code (UDC), staff recommends approval of the final plat with the
following conditions:
1) The words “subdivision” and “City of” are removed from the title block;
2) The donation statements are updated to donate the land to the City of
Cheyenne, not the Parks and Recreation Department; and
3) Complete an agreement with WyDOT concerning future cost sharing when
traffic control improvements at US Highway 30 and Whitney Road are
warranted.
```

### Page 5 of 9

```text
Final Plat/UDC-15-00425
4
PLAT DATA:  In reviewing, providing comments, and making recommendations and decisions on a
proposed preliminary plat, the Staff, Planning Commission and City Council shall use the following
criteria:

COMPLIANCE WITH APPROVED PRELIMINARY PLAT - The layout and design of the final plat is in substantial
compliance with the approved preliminary plat considering the number of lots or parcels, street and block
layout, and access, and any deviations in the final plat brings the application in further compliance with
the Comprehensive Plan.

The submitted final plat is substantially similar to the approved preliminary plat. There are ninety
(90) residential lots over the 15+ acre area. These residential lots are served by two rights-of-
way that are extended from the existing Saddle Ridge and two newly constructed cul-de-sacs.
The donated land is also the same as shown on the preliminary plat. Multiple requests have been
made to donate the land generally to the City of Cheyenne instead of calling out specific
departments. As of the mailing of this report, the plat is still specifically donating the land to the
Parks and Recreation Department. The Parks and Recreation Depart has also expressed that they
are not interested in taking Lot 1, Block 1 without further amenities added to it. Discussions for
further amenities have not been had and other City departments are not interested in
maintaining the lot.

CONDITIONS OF PRELIMINARY PLAT - The application meets any conditions or modifications of the
approved preliminary plat.

There were not any conditions placed on the preliminary plat. As of the mailing of this staff
report, the modifications requested by City Staff have not been made to the map.

STANDARDS - Division of land meets the purposes, intent, and all applicable standards in Article 4.

Transportation Network:
This plat has proposed to extend two existing streets, Campfire Trail and Horse Soldier, while
constructing two new cul-de-sacs, Wilderness Court and Painted Rock Trail. The two blocks meet
the standards as required by Article 4 of the UDC for connections. As noted during the
preliminary plat phase by multiple departments, two additional connections to existing rights-of-
way are recommended for the number of units proposed but are not required. It is expected that
all streets will comply with the current standards set forth in Section 4.3.4 of the UDC, including
detached sidewalk construction and tree lawn placement.
Community Facility Fees:
Park Fees:
As outlined in Section 4.2.5.c.2&3 and Table 4-1, UDC, Park fees are calculated as follows:
Total Plat Area: 15.7 acres
(less) ROW Area: 3.2 acres
(less) Dedicated Area: 1.5 acres
(equals) Park Fee Subject Area: 11 acres

Land Acquisition Fee = $350 x 11 or $3,850
Infrastructure Fee = $350 x 11 or $3,850
(less) Previously Paid Fees = $500 x 15.7 = $7,850
```

### Page 6 of 9

```text
Final Plat/UDC-15-00425
5
Due to previously paid Community Facility Fees for the subject area, the applicant does not owe
any Land Acquisition or Infrastructure fees. Though not owing the previously mentioned fees, the
applicant will be subject to the Residential Enhancement Fee of $400 per Dwelling Unit (DU). This
fee is payable at the issuance of each building permit. With the proposed 90 lots, the estimated
Enhancement Fee for this project is $36,000.

Open Space:
With this development, a portion of the property is within a ¼ mile of the Saddle Ridge Park to
the south. This, along with proximity to Saddle Ridge Elementary and future proposed Greenway,
allows this subdivision to meet exceptions for requiring open space. The applicant has proposed
dedicating Lot 1, Block 1 to the Parks & Recreation Department for greenway extension. The
Parks & Recreation Department has said they are open to the idea but would like to see
amenities added to the lot before they accept it. Other departments within the City have
expressed they do not want to maintain Lot 1, Block 1. The developer has also proposed
dedicating Lot 1, Block 2 to the Parks & Recreation Department but the lot would be used for
drainage purposes. The Parks & Recreation Department has stated they are not interested in Lot
1, Block 2 since there is no recreational value. The Engineering Department has stated they will
maintain the lot since it is used for drainage.

SURROUNDING DEVELOPMENT - The application will not create any undue influence on existing or potential
development for adjacent property considering the existing zoning and Comprehensive Plan policies for
that property.

This development should not create any undue influence on the surrounding area considering
existing zoning. While the future land use map would not match, the submitted amendment
request, if approved, would amend the future land use map to Urban Residential for the subject
area.

Reviewing Agencies w/ Comments:
-
Board of Public Utilities (Linda Gunter) (FYI)
o
With the requested zone change from two commercial lots to ninety residential lots. The
Owner / Developer is responsible for verifying the remaining available sewer capacity of
the existing sewer system in the area to serve this project.
-
City Surveyor (Jack Studley) (FYI)
o
Boundary data and legal descriptions appear in order.
-
County Assessor (Clarice Blanton) (Developer Plans to Donate Lots Instead of Dedicate)
o
Would prefer to see under PLAT NOTES #1 and 2, ‘...is hereby dedicated to the City of
Cheyenne...’ instead of ’to be donated to’. With the current wording I would leave the
ownership under Saddle Ridge LLC until a deed was recorded transferring ownership to
the City of Cheyenne.
-
Development Department (Josh Tetzlaff) (Correct Prior to Signing)
o
Please remove the words “subdivision” and “City of” from the legal description in the title
block.
o
Plat Note #1: Parks & Rec stated in previous comments (see preliminary plat comments)
that they are not interested in Lot 1, Block 1 without amenities being added. Please
contact Parks & Rec to clear up.
o
Plat Note #2: Lot 1, Block 2 should be donated to the Engineering Department for
drainage purposes. Please correct on plat.
-
Laramie County School District #1 (Chris Hout) (FYI)
```

